# Beat for demo step "Hidden bits": the covert channel reveal (spec 0396).
#
# Drives protolens through one captured *response* that carries "whoami" in
# the hidden channel. One bit per VARINT field, in document order: the grid
# is field 1, so its cells come first, from row 1, cell 1 — a padded varint
# is a 1, a minimal one a 0, most significant bit first. Cells 1 and 2 start
# the `w` (01110111): cell 1 canonical, cell 2 the first spurious
# continuation byte.
#
# The beat opens on the whole message, shows the first two cells side by
# side at the wire level, then selects the cells of each letter in turn —
# one step per letter, with an ASCII table — and ends on the reassembled
# word. "whoami" is 48 bits, so every letter sits in row 1 as long as the
# grid is at least 48 cells wide: Bob's window at least 96 columns (each
# Life cell is two columns). Advance with space or Enter; the presenter may
# also move by hand.
#
# Step text is written for a 120-column line (spec 0400).
#
# Invocation (from grehack2026.sh), on the flagged response capture:
#   protolens --descriptor-set life.desc capture/NNNNNN-response.pb \
#       --script beats/smuggle

title: The smuggled bits

steps:
  - text: |
      One captured response, in full. protolens flags fields that do
      not sit the way the schema expects — spurious padding on varints.

      standard tools see nothing: the padding does not change the decoded value.

      👋 Press space to step onto the first two cells.
    node: /

  - text: |
      The grid is just cells. Here are the first row's first two cells, at the wire
      level (`w`). The first — highlighted — is a plain, minimal varint: nothing hidden.

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/1
    wire_lines:
      from: /1/1/1
      to: /1/1/2
    search: /1/1/1

  - text: |
      The second cell — now highlighted — decodes to the same value, but the wire shows an extra byte: a
      continuation byte (high bit set) then a zero. Legal, non-minimal, and that extra byte carries one hidden bit.

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/2
    wire_lines:
      from: /1/1/1
      to: /1/1/2
    search: /1/1/2

  - text: |
      Eight cells, eight bits — the first byte, selected end to end. The bytes are lowercase ASCII: 01110111 → w.

        a 01100001    b 01100010    c 01100011    d 01100100    e 01100101    f 01100110    g 01100111
        h 01101000    i 01101001    j 01101010    k 01101011    l 01101100    m 01101101    n 01101110
        o 01101111    p 01110000    q 01110001    r 01110010    s 01110011    t 01110100    u 01110101
        v 01110110    w 01110111    x 01111000    y 01111001    z 01111010

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/1
    select_lines:
      from: /1/1/1
      to: /1/1/8

  - text: |
      The next eight cells → 01101000 → h.

        a 01100001    b 01100010    c 01100011    d 01100100    e 01100101    f 01100110    g 01100111
        h 01101000    i 01101001    j 01101010    k 01101011    l 01101100    m 01101101    n 01101110
        o 01101111    p 01110000    q 01110001    r 01110010    s 01110011    t 01110100    u 01110101
        v 01110110    w 01110111    x 01111000    y 01111001    z 01111010

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/9
    select_lines:
      from: /1/1/9
      to: /1/1/16

  - text: |
      The next eight → 01101111 → o.

        a 01100001    b 01100010    c 01100011    d 01100100    e 01100101    f 01100110    g 01100111
        h 01101000    i 01101001    j 01101010    k 01101011    l 01101100    m 01101101    n 01101110
        o 01101111    p 01110000    q 01110001    r 01110010    s 01110011    t 01110100    u 01110101
        v 01110110    w 01110111    x 01111000    y 01111001    z 01111010

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/17
    select_lines:
      from: /1/1/17
      to: /1/1/24

  - text: |
      The next eight → 01100001 → a.

        a 01100001    b 01100010    c 01100011    d 01100100    e 01100101    f 01100110    g 01100111
        h 01101000    i 01101001    j 01101010    k 01101011    l 01101100    m 01101101    n 01101110
        o 01101111    p 01110000    q 01110001    r 01110010    s 01110011    t 01110100    u 01110101
        v 01110110    w 01110111    x 01111000    y 01111001    z 01111010

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/25
    select_lines:
      from: /1/1/25
      to: /1/1/32

  - text: |
      The next eight → 01101101 → m.

        a 01100001    b 01100010    c 01100011    d 01100100    e 01100101    f 01100110    g 01100111
        h 01101000    i 01101001    j 01101010    k 01101011    l 01101100    m 01101101    n 01101110
        o 01101111    p 01110000    q 01110001    r 01110010    s 01110011    t 01110100    u 01110101
        v 01110110    w 01110111    x 01111000    y 01111001    z 01111010

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/33
    select_lines:
      from: /1/1/33
      to: /1/1/40

  - text: |
      The next eight → 01101001 → i.

        a 01100001    b 01100010    c 01100011    d 01100100    e 01100101    f 01100110    g 01100111
        h 01101000    i 01101001    j 01101010    k 01101011    l 01101100    m 01101101    n 01101110
        o 01101111    p 01110000    q 01110001    r 01110010    s 01110011    t 01110100    u 01110101
        v 01110110    w 01110111    x 01111000    y 01111001    z 01111010

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/41
    select_lines:
      from: /1/1/41
      to: /1/1/48

  - text: |
      Read in order, the six bytes spell Eve's command:

        01110111 01101000 01101111 01100001 01101101 01101001
           w        h        o        a        m        i      → whoami

      This is a response, so it carries Eve's command. The client's next request carries its output back the same way.

      👋 Type `:q` to quit protolens.
